Cushing's Syndrome
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Central obesity/Moon facies, Violaceous abdominal striae, Proximal muscle weakness, Failure to suppress on dexamethasone test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-007
Reason for Visit: Acute functional decline, inability to mobilize from a seated position, and severe, uncharacteristic mood lability.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe deterioration in mobility rendering the patient unable to stand, combined with sudden, profound behavioral changes and a hypertensive reading on a home monitor. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was mandated to rule out acute neurological events, severe hypokalaemia, or hypertensive crisis before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I literally cannot lift my legs to get out of this chair, and my head feels like it's going to explode with pressure."
History of Present Illness: Over the past 72 hours, the patient reports a rapid worsening of proximal muscle weakness, progressing to an inability to rise from a chair or climb stairs tonight. This is superimposed on a chronic history of insidious weight gain focused centrally, easy bruising, and extremely thin, fragile skin. The family notes the patient has developed a plethoric, rounded face over recent months. Concurrently, the adult child reports severe mood lability and uncharacteristic agitation over the last two days. The patient confirms a severe, pounding headache and a home blood pressure reading of 190/110 mmHg earlier this evening.
Functional Status: Acutely chair-bound due to profound proximal myopathy.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk if attempting to mobilize unassisted. Severe mood lability is distressing to the family unit.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/105 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98 percent on room air | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Agitated, plethoric, and demonstrating central adiposity with remarkably thin extremities.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals profound proximal muscle weakness (power 3/5 in hip flexors and shoulder abductors). Dermatological exam notes broad violaceous striae across the abdomen and multiple ecchymoses on the forearms with paper-thin skin. Head and neck exam reveals a moon facies and a prominent dorsocervical fat pad. Cardiovascular exam confirms severe resting hypertension. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Central obesity/Moon facies, Violaceous abdominal striae, Proximal muscle weakness, and anticipated Failure to suppress on dexamethasone test pending future formal endocrine workup.
Environmental Observations: Rugs in the living area present a significant trip hazard given the patient's acute lower limb weakness.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Denies exogenous corticosteroid use (oral, topical, or inhaled).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cushing's Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, bilateral proximal weakness without focal cranial nerve deficits, sensory loss, or aphasia.

Exogenous steroid-induced myopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The explicit denial of any current or recent prescribed or over-the-counter corticosteroid use.

Essential hypertension - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The overt presence of characteristic somatic changes, profound myopathy, and broad purple striae indicating a secondary endocrine etiology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe proximal myopathy causing functional immobility, combined with poorly controlled, severe hypertension and sudden mood lability. Telephone triage accurately recognized the risk of acute hypertensive end-organ damage or an acute hypokalaemic event frequently associated with hypercortisolism. The objective confirmation of severe hypertension and chair-bound status mandated immediate clinical intervention to initiate blood pressure control and formulate a safe mobilization plan. Delaying this assessment risked hypertensive emergency or a severe traumatic fall during the unmonitored overnight period, strictly fulfilling the urgent MBS item criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated oral amlodipine 5mg to cautiously manage severe hypertension. Requested urgent morning laboratory panels including comprehensive metabolic panel (to exclude severe hypokalaemia), morning cortisol, and ACTH.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically sliding to the edge of the chair and using your arms to push off rather than relying on your thighs (action), you redistribute the mechanical load (cause), directly allowing you to stand without collapsing from muscle weakness (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent community physiotherapy referral for mobility assessment and safe transfer techniques.
DME/Safety (Optional): Prescription of a raised toilet seat and a sturdy walking frame for immediate use.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to monitor blood pressure response and coordinate urgent endocrinology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the headache worsens, vision changes occur, chest pain develops, or the patient becomes acutely confused, present to the emergency department immediately or call 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This clinical picture strongly suggests an excess of cortisol in the body, requiring specialized blood and urine tests to confirm the source.
Clinical Course: The muscle weakness and blood pressure issues will persist until the underlying hormonal overproduction is definitively treated, usually via surgery or targeted medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk without the physical assistance of your family member tonight. If you feel sudden chest pressure or severe shortness of breath, call an ambulance immediately.

